{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And HaShem planted… and He placed</b> etc. <b>and He caused to grow</b> etc. The reason He interrupted the account of the planting of the Garden with the placing of the man there is to say that for his sake HaShem created the Garden and for his honor. And this is the measure of the verse: <b>and He planted</b> etc. <b>and He placed</b> — meaning, for this reason, to place the man there — and afterward He returned to detail the matters of the Garden and its rivers and their functions. And He returned to say (Genesis 2:15) <b>and HaShem took</b> etc. <b>and He caused him to rest</b> etc., to reveal what we have explained in His saying ״וַיָּשֶׂם״ — that He intended nothing by it other than that the man was the reason for planting the Garden, for if it were about informing us of the placing of the man in the Garden, behold He says ״וַיִּקַּח״ etc. ״וַיַּנִּחֵהוּ״. And the verse ״וַיַּנִּחֵהוּ״ alone would not suffice to convey this — that for the man, for his sake alone, HaShem created the Garden — for it is possible that HaShem created it to place therein whomever is upright in His eyes, for His ministers, for His angels, for the supernal spirits, and at that time it pleased Him that the man too would be there, and on the very day he was placed there he was driven out and sent away (as is known from the text), and the Garden remained for the rest of His creatures. Therefore HaShem made known His kindness, that the Garden He created for the man, and it still stands for the man, the beloved of his Maker, and no created being has any portion in the Garden except for the man who did not blemish His likeness and His image."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:38:30.429313153Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}